1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:05,573
A reservation is a promise: a place, a time, and agreed terms.

2
00:00:05,813 --> 00:00:12,733
If a restaurant table becomes unusable before dinner, keeping
that promise can mean changing several bookings together.

3
00:00:12,973 --> 00:00:17,343
This is Tablekeeper: keep the
promise, even when the floor changes.

4
00:00:17,823 --> 00:00:22,127
Behind it is Proofline, a software factory in BAND Desktop.

5
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:32,740
Shivam Gupta directed the product and configured four coding-agent
seats: Coordinator, Engineer, Experience and Verifier.

6
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:35,063
The agents wrote and checked the implementation.

7
00:00:35,303 --> 00:00:38,873
Their standing mandates describe reusable responsibilities.

8
00:00:39,113 --> 00:00:41,603
Restaurant requirements belong in the task.

9
00:00:42,083 --> 00:00:43,867
This is the real production room.

10
00:00:44,107 --> 00:00:46,877
One dispatch covered four stages.

11
00:00:47,117 --> 00:00:54,077
Engineer and Experience shared implementation work,
while Verifier checked committed revisions independently.

12
00:00:54,317 --> 00:00:58,457
The handoffs connect decisions to
the code and evidence they produced.

13
00:00:58,937 --> 00:01:01,980
That separation caught something the published checks missed.

14
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:08,320
In stage three, a corrupted restore could erase the permanent
exception on an individually changed recurring booking.

15
00:01:08,560 --> 00:01:10,620
Verifier rejected the candidate.

16
00:01:10,860 --> 00:01:13,603
The Engineer seat fixed the history validation.

17
00:01:13,843 --> 00:01:18,033
Independent checks then passed,
including the original regression.

18
00:01:18,273 --> 00:01:21,187
The failed review and fix remain inspectable.

19
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:25,307
The run also needed operator help after a daemon interruption.

20
00:01:25,547 --> 00:01:30,270
The same BAND seats restarted in the same
room and acquired new provider sessions.

21
00:01:30,510 --> 00:01:33,183
No new task or implementation hints were sent.

22
00:01:33,423 --> 00:01:36,767
This was a single-dispatch run, with recovery assistance.

23
00:01:37,247 --> 00:01:39,170
Now look at the manager preview.

24
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:42,673
These are three synthetic bookings at The Orangery.

25
00:01:42,913 --> 00:01:47,917
Closing Window nook proposes one move
to Garden table, with zero unused seats.

26
00:01:48,157 --> 00:01:49,880
The other bookings stay put.

27
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:54,970
Guests keep their arrival times, party sizes and accepted terms.

28
00:01:55,210 --> 00:01:56,713
Nothing has moved yet.

29
00:01:56,953 --> 00:01:59,703
The manager reviews before applying the plan.

30
00:02:00,183 --> 00:02:06,033
The planner supports up to six tables, four
declared pairs and six considered bookings.

31
00:02:06,273 --> 00:02:10,003
Every confirmed booking overlapping the closure interval counts.

32
00:02:10,243 --> 00:02:16,283
A stale preview must fail safely, and an
impossible plan cannot silently cancel a guest.

33
00:02:16,763 --> 00:02:23,313
All four original stages passed their required published
checks in both isolated and fresh-clone isolated runs.

34
00:02:23,553 --> 00:02:26,653
Stage four passed one hundred and fifty-eight checks.

35
00:02:26,893 --> 00:02:32,003
Independent verification also passed sixty-nine HTTP checks.

36
00:02:32,243 --> 00:02:35,643
These are finite tests, not production certification.

37
00:02:36,123 --> 00:02:39,623
The hosted companion is separate work from that graded run.

38
00:02:39,863 --> 00:02:45,443
The first customer hypothesis is small
venues with direct or recurring groups.

39
00:02:45,683 --> 00:02:50,343
Seventy-nine dollars per location per
month is a price to test, not revenue.

40
00:02:50,583 --> 00:02:52,947
Tablekeeper keeps guest promises.

41
00:02:53,187 --> 00:02:56,747
Proofline makes the work behind those promises inspectable.
